# -*- mode: python ; coding: utf-8 -*-
"""DRAGONBYTE-Z-SHIP-V1-001 - the frozen Windows build of DragonByte Z.

The third product this repository freezes, and the one that is an application
rather than a randomizer. It reuses the established shape rather than inventing a
second one: the same reduced Qt payload, the same build-time identity record, the
same ``datas`` entry that makes the ROM profile catalog travel, and the same
generated-at-build-time ``.spec`` beside a tracked template.

**Onedir**, not one file. The randomizers are one file because their release is a
single executable a user double-clicks; an editor that carries a Qt runtime, a
profile catalog and a project loader is a directory that starts in well under a
second, and the sibling template already records the measured four-second
one-file startup cost this avoids. ``EXE(exclude_binaries=True)`` plus ``COLLECT``
is therefore the shape here, and the release artefact is
``dist/DragonByte Z/DragonByte Z.exe``.

What travels, and what does not
-------------------------------

``tools/webfoot/data/roms.yaml`` is the only bundled data file. It is a table of
addresses and hashes, never game content, and ``tools.webfoot.catalog`` falls
back to it in a frozen build - so without it every ROM selection is refused.

The assembled atlas is deliberately NOT bundled. It is derived from the user's own
image and cannot be shipped, and :func:`tools.webfoot.cli._default_atlas_directory`
is the one authority on where a frozen build looks for it: beside the executable,
under ``build/atlas``, which is exactly where a generated artefact belongs.

Identity
--------

``scripts/randomizer.ico`` is reused rather than a second icon minted: it is the
repository's own mark, rendered from ``public/favicon.svg``, and it is the icon
both released tools already carry.

The executable carries the product's own identity in two forms: the Windows
version resource named below, whose fields a stranger reads in the file's
properties dialog, and the runtime string ``--version`` prints, which comes from
``tools/webfoot/release_identity.py`` by way of the build-time record this template
bakes in before PyInstaller runs. ``tests/unit/test_dragonbyte_release.py`` holds
the two to the same version, so neither can drift into a second identity.
"""

#: Python modules nothing here imports. PySide6's PyInstaller hook collects the
#: whole Qt SDK otherwise, and a single-window editor loads none of these.
_UNUSED_QT_MODULES = [
    'PySide6.QtQml',
    'PySide6.QtQuick',
    'PySide6.QtQuickWidgets',
    'PySide6.QtPdf',
    'PySide6.QtPdfWidgets',
    'PySide6.QtVirtualKeyboard',
]

#: Native libraries and data belonging to those modules, matched on their
#: bundle-relative path with forward slashes.
_UNUSED_QT_PREFIXES = (
    'PySide6/Qt6Qml',
    'PySide6/Qt6Quick',
    'PySide6/Qt6Pdf',
    'PySide6/Qt6VirtualKeyboard',
    'PySide6/translations/',
)


def _is_unused_qt(relative: str) -> bool:
    normalised = relative.replace('\\', '/')
    return normalised.startswith(_UNUSED_QT_PREFIXES)


a = Analysis(
    ['tools/dragonbyte/_pyinstaller_entry.py'],
    pathex=['.'],
    binaries=[],
    datas=[('tools/webfoot/data/roms.yaml', 'tools/webfoot/data')],
    # The profile catalog is reached through a *string*
    # `importlib.resources.files("tools.webfoot.data")`, which PyInstaller cannot
    # follow. Naming the package makes its inclusion a declaration rather than a
    # coincidence.
    hiddenimports=['tools.webfoot.data'],
    hookspath=[],
    hooksconfig={},
    runtime_hooks=[],
    excludes=_UNUSED_QT_MODULES,
    noarchive=False,
    optimize=0,
)

a.binaries = [item for item in a.binaries if not _is_unused_qt(item[0])]
a.datas = [item for item in a.datas if not _is_unused_qt(item[0])]

pyz = PYZ(a.pure)

exe = EXE(
    pyz,
    a.scripts,
    [],
    exclude_binaries=True,
    name='DragonByte Z',
    icon='scripts/randomizer.ico',
    version='DragonByte Z.version.txt',
    debug=False,
    bootloader_ignore_signals=False,
    strip=False,
    upx=True,
    console=False,
    disable_windowed_traceback=False,
    argv_emulation=False,
    target_arch=None,
    codesign_identity=None,
    entitlements_file=None,
)

coll = COLLECT(
    exe,
    a.binaries,
    a.datas,
    strip=False,
    upx=True,
    upx_exclude=[],
    name='DragonByte Z',
)
